name:
  type: string
  unique: true
  id: true
  description: Unique name for the cluster

version:
  type: string
  default: v1
  description: Schema version for this cluster definition

hosts:
  type: custom
  arity: 1..*
  description: Hosts composing the cluster
  schema: host.schema

features:
  type: custom
  arity: 0..*
  description: |
    Cluster features to activate.

    Each entry may be an inline feature definition **or** a reference to an
    external feature file.  Three forms are supported:

    1. **Inline** — full definition in the cluster YAML (current behaviour):
         - name: monitoring
           labels: [primary, compute]
           install-cmds:
             - helm install ...

    2. **By URI** — load definition from a separate file:
         - uri: ./features/monitoring.yaml

       Any additional inline fields (e.g. `name`) override the values from
       the referenced file.

    3. **By name** — shorthand that looks up `features/<name>.yaml` relative
       to the cluster file:
         - name: monitoring

       Equivalent to `uri: ./features/monitoring.yaml` without the explicit
       path.  Use this convention when all your feature files live in a
       `features/` directory next to the cluster YAML.

    Forms 2 and 3 make feature definitions reusable across multiple clusters.
  schema: feature.schema

config:
  type: filesystem
  arity: 0..*
  description: |
    Local filesystem paths containing supplementary
    configuration files made available during deployment

translations:
  type: custom
  arity: 0..*
  description: |
    Translation recipes that allow heterogeneous backend types to coexist
    in a single cluster definition.

    A cluster whose hosts all use the same ``backend.type`` does NOT need
    translation recipes — the homogeneous case is the default and is always
    valid.

    When hosts with *different* ``backend.type`` values are declared, a
    recipe must exist for **every distinct (source, target) pair** among
    those types.  Both directions are usually required because join-token
    exchange is asymmetric.

    IMPORTANT — intrinsic limitations:

      k0s and Docker Swarm are architecturally incompatible.  They use
      different control planes (etcd + Kubernetes API server vs. Raft in
      the Docker daemon), incompatible join protocols, and separate overlay
      networks (CNI plugins vs. Docker VXLAN).  Nodes running different
      backends cannot form a single native cluster.

      A translation recipe is the *declaration* that a bridge will be
      provided; the actual bridging logic must be implemented as a
      BackendTranslator registered with the DeployOrchestrator at deploy
      time.  No concrete translator is shipped yet — this is an extension
      point for future federation work (e.g. a cross-cluster service mesh,
      API gateway, or custom network overlay).

    Example::

        translations:
          - source: k0s
            target: swarm
          - source: swarm
            target: k0s

  schema:
    source:
      type: string
      choice: ["k0s", "swarm"]
      description: |
        The backend type that initiates the connection / sends the join token.
    target:
      type: string
      choice: ["k0s", "swarm"]
      description: |
        The backend type that receives the connection / consumes the join token.

  